QUOTE_VERBATIM:
HTTP Connection Request Smuggling

{{#include ../banners/hacktricks-training.md}}

HTTP connection request smuggling is a connection-state / routing problem rather than a classic CL.TE/TE.CL parser discrepancy.

RFC 9113 is stricter than many downgrade implementations:
 * connection-specific headers such as transfer-encoding, upgrade, keep-alive, or proxy-connection make the HTTP/2 message malformed;
 * te is only valid as te: trailers;
 * if content-length is present, it must match the sum of the DATA frame payload lengths.

Request Smuggling in HTTP/2 Downgrades

{{#include ../../banners/hacktricks-training.md}}

HTTP/2 is generally considered immune to classic request-smuggling because the length of each DATA frame is explicit.
